      "math_academy_id": "e-1386",
      "problem": "Express $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{\\sqrt{z+1}}$ without radicals in the denominator.",
      "worked_solution": "We need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{z+1}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{z+1}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{\\sqrt{z+1}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{z+1}}{\\sqrt{z+1}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(\\sqrt{y}+2)\\sqrt{z+1}}{(\\sqrt{z+1})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{y}\\cdot \\sqrt{z+1}+2\\cdot \\sqrt{z+1}}{z+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{y\\cdot (z+1)}+2\\sqrt{z+1}}{z+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{yz+y}+2\\sqrt{z+1}}{z+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
